THE READER RULE (the gate's, for every PR whose change READS text or output and turns it into a verdict — #1253's cell 9 over the subject's source and cell 7 over its own bytes; #1254's derivedTreeWalkers() over the test sources; #1250's `t1302_dir_of` over the runner's note and its KS-1303 control over the verdict line; #1252's dedicated-verb read over the registry). A shape counts as REAL when it occurs in the tree OR the PR's own commit / cell text NAMES it as a shape the check catches. A WRONG READING ON ANY REAL SHAPE = NO GO for that PR (blocking): name the shape, quote the line, describe the fix-shape and the regression cell. A wrong reading only on a shape that neither occurs nor is named is graded by REACH (non-blocking unless you show reach). Run every reading THROUGH THE REAL CODE PATH — never a lifted regex, never a Python port: for #1253, a GATE-PROBE copy of the subject in YOUR worktree with ONE call site rewritten per shape (`build_fixture "$WORK/c0" with-develop | cat` first — the shape the commit names; then `&`, backticks, a `(` on its own line, `{ …; } | cat`, and the safe `false || build_fixture …`), the fixture guard run with `SUBJ_SH=<copy>` so cell 9 reads it, each result quoted; for #1254, a GATE-PROBE walker file in a SUB-directory and one reading through `readdir` from node:fs/promises, `walkTimeouts.test.ts` run over each. The drafter's port predicts #1253 cell 9 MISSES `bf | cat`: if the real suite confirms, THE READER RULE makes #1253 NO GO — say so plainly, AND say the reach (grep the subject and every other `*.test.sh` for a `build_fixture` call with a trailing pipe or `&`) and the cost, so Wednesday can weigh it; do not soften the rule yourself. If the real run reads it correctly, CONFIRMED for the seat — say why and correct the drafter.

